Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Hundimiento de San Jerónimo
- Story & Context: When an unauthorized blast sheared the hydraulic supports at Level 14, drill boss Tadeo Vaca found himself buried in complete darkness with nothing but the taste of bitter copper dust and the shrill sound of escaping air. Grabbing an adjustable wrench, he began striking the intake tube in a frantic 3-2 dembow syncopation to signal the hoist operator, accidentally improvising the EP's foundational rhythm while muttering prayers through his cracked respirator. His crew survived five days by matching their breathing to his mechanical bongo pattern, producing a track that captures pure survival adrenaline: "Respira hondo que el zinc no miente."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro shot of a miner's soot-covered calloused hand striking a bent steel wrench against a massive corrugated zinc pipe in suffocating darkness, tiny sparks illuminating floating quartz dust and rusted rivets, cinematic high-contrast documentary style.

Track 2: "¿Quién Marcó la Cota Cero?"
- Story & Context: Written during an illegal night shift beneath the contested border zone of the Fila Seca ridgeline, underground surveyor Marisol Peña whispered this confessional verse into a broken dispatch mic while cold saline condensation dripped steadily down her neck. She had intentionally misdrawn the government survey maps to hide an illicit cobalt vein, mapping the subterranean acoustics through hollow bongo rolls bounced down abandoned ventilation flues. The track layers her haunting, monotone flow over the clicking rhythm of air regulators and muffled subterranean percussion, transforming cartographic fraud into an intimate ghost story: "Entre la cota y el infierno dejé mi nombre colgado."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An antique, yellowed topographic survey map pinned to damp stone underground, partially burned along the contour lines, illuminated by a flickering emerald mining flare, showing hand-drawn red borders disappearing into an abyss.

Track 3: Compass Needles Chew the Zinc
- Story & Context: Long after the San Jerónimo pit was sealed with ten tons of poured concrete and expunged from regional transit registries, scrap scavengers noticed that magnetic instruments violently spun whenever they approached the rusted ventilation caps protruding from the desert floor. Lowering an omnidirectional hydrophone down the flue, producer Yulissa Ramos recorded the abandoned borehole still playing back the acoustic resonance of dead shifts, layering fresh bongo slaps over the spectral, metallic drone of the mountain itself. The resulting track became an urban legend among border truckers, who swear the desert floor hums with bass at dusk: "El mapa se borró pero la roca sigue escupiendo."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A solitary rusted exhaust pipe sticking out of cracked, arid desert terrain at twilight, glowing faintly from within with an unearthly pale white light, magnetic compasses scattered on the ground around it spinning erratically, surreal moody cinematic realism.

